</figure><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span>Different versions of the model exist for individual modalities, but no unified model for multiple modalities.</span></span></span>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Although inference efficiency is essential for model deployment, pretraining efficiency is equally critical given the scale of recent <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RS</abbr> <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FM</abbr>.
Reducing the dataset size by eliminating redundant samples is therefore essential to improve training efficiency and scalability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>.
Training on datasets comprising hundreds of billions of pixels introduces substantial engineering and computational challenges <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.
Efficient storage, loading, and throughput must be ensured to prevent data pipeline bottlenecks, which often require specialized infrastructure.
In addition to these computational challenges, training datasets can contain redundant samples, such as images from the same  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">LULC</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">LULC</abbr>) class under the same climate zone (e.g., extensive deserts or forests) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.
When included in the training data, these additional samples may not contribute significant new information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>.
Therefore, when the considered models are computationally intensive, a high amount of redundancies may increase the training costs without improving the capability of the learned representations, resulting in minor improvements relative to the increasing computational demands.
Recent studies have begun to address similar challenges in general machine learning through automatic data selection and curation strategies (e.g., graph-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> and clustering-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> approaches).
In the context of large-scale <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RS</abbr> <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FM</abbr>, however, such strategies remain underexplored and are not yet widely established.
In practice, the prevailing paradigm still assumes that increasing the size of the pretraining dataset uniformly translates into better performance, often without explicitly accounting for redundancy.
The above-mentioned issues result in: i) the computational inefficiency of current <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RS</abbr> <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FM</abbr> during pretraining and inference; and ii) the absence of efficient and widely established data selection strategies to mitigate redundancies in pretraining data for <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RS</abbr> <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FM</abbr>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To address these issues and achieve efficiency in training and inference, we propose an approach that aims to inject a <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> into a <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RS</abbr> <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FM</abbr>.
Although prior studies have explored the use of <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> in <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RS</abbr> model development <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, these works primarily employ <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> as a means to scale model capacity, rather than to explicitly target computational efficiency.
In contrast, our focus is on leveraging <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> to enhance efficiency, for which we adopt the Soft <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, a variant designed to combine high representational capacity with reduced computational cost.
When Soft <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> are applied in transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, each input token is softly routed to multiple expert branches, and their outputs are combined using the learned routing weights.
The mechanism is particularly well-suited for efficient <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RS</abbr> <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FM</abbr>, as it combines a lightweight routing mechanism with soft token mixing, thereby increasing representational capacity while reducing computational complexity.
We inject the Soft <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> into the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMAE</abbr> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FM</abbr> to create our <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FM</abbr> that we call <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMoE</abbr>.
In addition, we introduce an efficient thematic-climatic descriptor-driven sampling strategy that selects a representative training set from a large-scale image archive, while retaining the full geographic and thematic-climatic diversity of the original archive.
To this end, our proposed thematic-climatic descriptor-driven sampling strategy consists of two stages:
The first stage aims at preserving thematic-climatic diversity while reducing the sample size.
This is achieved by assigning each sample a combination of climatic and thematic descriptors (e.g., a climate zone and a set of <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">LULC</abbr> classes).
Then, a fixed number of samples is drawn from each combination of thematic-climatic descriptors to build an initial subsampled dataset.
In the second stage, the aim is to enhance the spatial diversity of the subset.
This is achieved by applying a genetic algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> that maximizes the geodesic distance within the selected samples.
Through these two stages, the strategy reduces redundancy by discarding semantically similar samples (e.g., multiple samples from the same combination of climate and thematic product descriptors) while maintaining the geographic and thematic-climatic diversity of the original archive.
This helps reduce the training time and environmental impact of <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FM</abbr> training in <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RS</abbr>.
We train our <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMoE</abbr> model using a subset of MajorTOM Core <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, where we select a training set using our thematic-climatic descriptor-driven sampling strategy.
Through extensive experiments, we demonstrate that our <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMoE</abbr> model archives performance compared to state-of-the-art <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FM</abbr>, while significantly reducing inference cost.
These results underscore <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMoE</abbr>’s efficiency and strong generalization capability across diverse remote sensing tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The main contributions of this work are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose the first computationally efficient <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FM</abbr> in <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RS</abbr>, <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMoE</abbr>, that injects a Soft <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> into <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMAE</abbr>.
As shown in <a href="#S1.T1" title="In I Introduction ‣ CSMoE: An Efficient Remote Sensing Foundation Model with Soft Mixture-of-Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>, this integration enhances the model’s representational capacity while significantly reducing computational complexity during both training and inference compared to models of similar size.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a new thematic-climatic descriptor-driven sampling strategy for sampling from large-scale, unlabeled <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RS</abbr> archives.
The strategy selects the optimal training set based on the user-defined sample count.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct extensive experiments on a suite of single- and multi-label scene classification and semantic segmentation (pixelwise classification) benchmarks, as well as both uni-modal and cross-modal <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CBIR</abbr> tasks in <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RS</abbr> and compare our <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMoE</abbr> model with state-of-the-art <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FM</abbr> in <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RS</abbr>.
We demonstrate that <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMoE</abbr> has a comparable or superior downstream performance while requiring fewer computational resources than competing models.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of this paper is organized as follows:
In <a href="#S2" title="II Proposed Efficient Remote Sensing Foundation Model with Soft Mixture-of-Experts ‣ CSMoE: An Efficient Remote Sensing Foundation Model with Soft Mixture-of-Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>, we introduce the proposed <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMoE</abbr> model and our efficient thematic-climatic descriptor-driven sampling strategy.
<a href="#S3" title="III Datasets and Experimental Setup ‣ CSMoE: An Efficient Remote Sensing Foundation Model with Soft Mixture-of-Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a> describes the experimental setup, including the construction of the pretraining dataset via the proposed sampling strategy, as well as the downstream datasets and implementation details.
<a href="#S4" title="IV Experimental Results ‣ CSMoE: An Efficient Remote Sensing Foundation Model with Soft Mixture-of-Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a> presents a comprehensive evaluation of the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMoE</abbr> model on scene classification, semantic segmentation, and image retrieval tasks, along with a sensitivity analysis.
Finally, <a href="#S5" title="V Conclusion ‣ CSMoE: An Efficient Remote Sensing Foundation Model with Soft Mixture-of-Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a> concludes the paper.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Proposed Efficient Remote Sensing Foundation Model with Soft Mixture-of-Experts</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> denote two co-registered multi-modal remote sensing image archives associated with different image modalities (i.e., acquired by different sensors).
The archives <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=\{\boldsymbol{x}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=\{\boldsymbol{x}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}=\{\boldsymbol{y}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}=\{\boldsymbol{y}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> each include <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> images, where <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are the <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RS</abbr> images in the respective archives and <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\boldsymbol{x}_{i},\boldsymbol{y}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\boldsymbol{x}_{i},\boldsymbol{y}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th multi-modal image pair that includes two images acquired by different sensors on the same geographical area.
We assume that an unlabeled training set <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}=\{(\boldsymbol{x}_{i},\boldsymbol{y}_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}=\{(\boldsymbol{x}_{i},\boldsymbol{y}_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is available for representation learning.
Although our approach can be extended to multiple modalities by applying the proposed processing steps in a pairwise combination, we focus on the dual-modality case to simplify the mathematical treatment in this paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The computational complexity of current <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RS</abbr> <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FM</abbr> during both training and inference poses significant challenges for operational deployment.
To address this limitation, we propose to integrate the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> mechanism into existing <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MAE</abbr>-based <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FM</abbr> architectures to achieve improved computational efficiency while maintaining or increasing representational capacity.
For representation learning from the training set <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MIM</abbr> (i.e., <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">SSL</abbr> through <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MAE</abbr>) has recently emerged as one of the most successful approaches in <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RS</abbr>.
Among various <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MAE</abbr> variants, the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMAE</abbr> model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> has shown promising results in both unimodal and cross-modal representation learning.
However, like all existing multi-modal <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MAE</abbr>-based <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FM</abbr>, <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMAE</abbr> suffers from a high computational complexity during training and inference with limited representational capacity.
As a first time in <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RS</abbr>, we explore the effectiveness of integrating the Soft <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> mechanism into <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MAE</abbr> to create computationally efficient <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FM</abbr>.
To this end, we select <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMAE</abbr> as our base model due to its proven cross-modal capabilities and apply our <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> injection adaptation to create <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMoE</abbr>.
In the following subsections, we first provide background information on the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMAE</abbr> model, and then present our adaptation that injects the Soft <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> into <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMAE</abbr> and our efficient thematic-climatic descriptor-driven sampling strategy.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Basics on Cross-Sensor Masked Autoencoder</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an image <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MAE</abbr> operate by dividing the image into <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> non-overlapping image patches of size <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\times\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\times\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, forming a token set <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}=\{\boldsymbol{p}_{n}\}_{n=1}^{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝒑</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>P</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}=\{\boldsymbol{p}_{n}\}_{n=1}^{P}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A random subset <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}\subset\{1,\ldots,P\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}\subset\{1,\ldots,P\}</annotation></semantics></math> is masked, while the remaining tokens <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}=\{1,\ldots,P\}\setminus\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}=\{1,\ldots,P\}\setminus\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> are passed to a transformer encoder <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> to produce latent representations <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}_{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}_{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}_{\mathcal{U}}=e(\{\boldsymbol{p}_{n}\}_{n\in\mathcal{U}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝒑</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}_{\mathcal{U}}=e(\{\boldsymbol{p}_{n}\}_{n\in\mathcal{U}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">These representations, along with a learned mask token <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{z}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{z}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, are used by a decoder <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> to get the reconstructed tokens <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{\boldsymbol{p}}_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒑</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{\boldsymbol{p}}_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{\boldsymbol{p}}_{n}\}=d(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathcal{U}},\boldsymbol{z}_{m})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒑</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{\boldsymbol{p}}_{n}\}=d(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathcal{U}},\boldsymbol{z}_{m}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The learning objective is computed over the masked positions on the reconstructed tokens:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{UMR}}=\text{RecL}(\{\hat{\boldsymbol{p}}_{n}\}_{n\in\mathcal{M}},\{\boldsymbol{p}_{n}\}_{n\in\mathcal{M}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>UMR</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>RecL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒑</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝒑</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{UMR}}=\text{RecL}(\{\hat{\boldsymbol{p}}_{n}\}_{n\in\mathcal{M}},\{\boldsymbol{p}_{n}\}_{n\in\mathcal{M}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <span id="S2.SS1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">RecL</span> is a reconstruction loss (e.g., mean average error or mean square error).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMAE</abbr> model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> extends this formulation by introducing two adaptations of <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MAE</abbr> to enable cross-modal representation learning:
i) extending the encoder into a multi-sensor encoder and a cross-sensor encoder; and
ii) extending the learning objective with latent similarity preservation and cross-modal reconstruction.
To accomplish this, first two independent masks <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> and their inverse <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> are created.
These are used on the input images, as described above, to get a set of tokens for each image modality.
Then, the multi-sensor encoder, which uses one sensor-specific  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ViT</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ViT</abbr>) encoder per modality, is applied to the unmasked patches.
Here, each sensor-specific encoder (<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{MS}^{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{MS}^{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{MS}^{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{MS}^{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>) has its own set of parameters, enabling the accurate modeling of sensor-specific image characteristics.
Then the cross-sensor encoder <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{CS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{CS}</annotation></semantics></math> (a <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ViT</abbr> encoder that uses shared weights among all modalities) is applied on the output of the multi-sensor encoder.
It aligns the latent representations of the different modalities into a shared embedding space by processing the output of the multi-sensor encoder as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{Z}_{\mathcal{U}}^{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{Z}_{\mathcal{U}}^{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=e_{CS}(e_{MS}^{\mathcal{X}}(\{\boldsymbol{p}^{\mathcal{X}}_{n}\}_{n\in\mathcal{U}^{\mathcal{X}}}))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mi>n</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=e_{CS}(e_{MS}^{\mathcal{X}}(\{\boldsymbol{p}^{\mathcal{X}}_{n}\}_{n\in\mathcal{U}^{\mathcal{X}}})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{Z}_{\mathcal{U}}^{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{Z}_{\mathcal{U}}^{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=e_{CS}(e_{MS}^{\mathcal{Y}}(\{\boldsymbol{p}^{\mathcal{Y}}_{n}\}_{n\in\mathcal{U}^{\mathcal{Y}}}))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mi>n</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=e_{CS}(e_{MS}^{\mathcal{Y}}(\{\boldsymbol{p}^{\mathcal{Y}}_{n}\}_{n\in\mathcal{U}^{\mathcal{Y}}})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To enable cross-modal alignment, the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMAE</abbr> model is trained using two cross-modal learning objectives:
i) cross-modal reconstruction; and
ii) latent similarity preservation.
For cross-modal reconstruction, two sensor-specific decoders <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> are employed.
Each decoder takes as input the features of one modality (<math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}_{\mathcal{U}}^{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}_{\mathcal{U}}^{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}_{\mathcal{U}}^{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}_{\mathcal{U}}^{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>) as well as a modality-specific mask token (<math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{z}^{\mathcal{X}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>m</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{z}^{\mathcal{X}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{z}^{\mathcal{Y}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>m</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{z}^{\mathcal{Y}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>) to reconstruct the masked patches of the other modality as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\{\hat{\boldsymbol{p}}^{\mathcal{Y}}_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒑</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\{\hat{\boldsymbol{p}}^{\mathcal{Y}}_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=d(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathcal{U}}^{\mathcal{X}},\boldsymbol{z}^{\mathcal{Y}}_{m})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>m</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=d(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathcal{U}}^{\mathcal{X}},\boldsymbol{z}^{\mathcal{Y}}_{m}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\{\hat{\boldsymbol{p}}^{\mathcal{X}}_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒑</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\{\hat{\boldsymbol{p}}^{\mathcal{X}}_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=d(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathcal{U}}^{\mathcal{Y}},\boldsymbol{z}^{\mathcal{X}}_{m})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>m</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=d(\mathcal{Z}_{\mathcal{U}}^{\mathcal{Y}},\boldsymbol{z}^{\mathcal{X}}_{m}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The cross-modal reconstruction loss <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{CMR}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>CMR</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{CMR}}</annotation></semantics></math> is then calculated using the same reconstruction loss as the single-modal reconstruction:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{CMR}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>CMR</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{CMR}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\text{RecL}(\{\hat{\boldsymbol{p}}^{\mathcal{Y}}_{n}\}_{n\in\mathcal{M}^{\mathcal{X}}},\{\boldsymbol{p}^{\mathcal{Y}}_{n}\}_{n\in\mathcal{M}^{\mathcal{X}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>RecL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒑</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mi>n</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\text{RecL}(\{\hat{\boldsymbol{p}}^{\mathcal{Y}}_{n}\}_{n\in\mathcal{M}^{\mathcal{X}}},\{\boldsymbol{p}^{\mathcal{Y}}_{n}\}_{n\in\mathcal{M}^{\mathcal{X}}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\text{RecL}(\{\hat{\boldsymbol{p}}^{\mathcal{X}}_{n}\}_{n\in\mathcal{M}^{\mathcal{Y}}},\{\boldsymbol{p}^{\mathcal{X}}_{n}\}_{n\in\mathcal{M}^{\mathcal{Y}}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mtext>RecL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒑</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mi>n</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\text{RecL}(\{\hat{\boldsymbol{p}}^{\mathcal{X}}_{n}\}_{n\in\mathcal{M}^{\mathcal{Y}}},\{\boldsymbol{p}^{\mathcal{X}}_{n}\}_{n\in\mathcal{M}^{\mathcal{Y}}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Note that here, for the loss calculation of one modality, the mask of the other modality is used, as this mask is also used to calculate the latent features.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For preservation of latent similarity, the mutual information loss <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{MI}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MI</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{MI}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, which is based on contrastive learning with normalized temperature-scaled cross-entropy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, is used.
For a batch of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{B}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{B}|</annotation></semantics></math> image pairs, let <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{c}_{i}^{\mathcal{X}},\boldsymbol{c}_{i}^{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒄</mi><mi>i</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒄</mi><mi>i</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{c}_{i}^{\mathcal{X}},\boldsymbol{c}_{i}^{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> be projected representations (e.g., projected <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[CLS]</span> tokens) that should be aligned in the latent space.
Then, using a similarity function <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sim}(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>sim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sim}(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., cosine similarity), a temperature parameter <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and the indicator function <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathds{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟙</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathds{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{MI}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MI</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{MI}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be defined as:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell^{i}(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell^{i}(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\text{log}\left(\frac{\exp{(\text{sim}(\boldsymbol{c}_{i}^{\mathcal{X}},\boldsymbol{c}_{i}^{\mathcal{Y}})/\tau})}{\sum_{q\in\mathcal{B}}\mathds{1}_{[q\neq i]}\exp{(\text{sim}(\boldsymbol{c}_{i}^{\mathcal{X}},\boldsymbol{c}_{q}^{\mathcal{Y}})/\tau})}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>log</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>sim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒄</mi><mi>i</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒄</mi><mi>i</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><mn>𝟙</mn><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>sim</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>q</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\text{log}\left(\frac{\exp{(\text{sim}(\boldsymbol{c}_{i}^{\mathcal{X}},\boldsymbol{c}_{i}^{\mathcal{Y}})/\tau})}{\sum_{q\in\mathcal{B}}\mathds{1}_{[q\neq i]}\exp{(\text{sim}(\boldsymbol{c}_{i}^{\mathcal{X}},\boldsymbol{c}_{q}^{\mathcal{Y}})/\tau})}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{MI}}(\mathcal{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MI</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{MI}}(\mathcal{B})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2|\mathcal{B}|}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{B}}\left(\ell^{i}(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y})+\ell^{i}(\mathcal{Y},\mathcal{X})\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2|\mathcal{B}|}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{B}}\left(\ell^{i}(\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y})+\ell^{i}(\mathcal{Y},\mathcal{X})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, the same learning objective <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{UMR}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>UMR</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{UMR}}</annotation></semantics></math> as in single-modal <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MAE</abbr> is applied on both modalities individually.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The proposed CSMoE Model</span></h3>

<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.14104v1/figures/images/CSMoE.drawio_v10.95.jpg" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:467/171;" width="467" height="171" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 1</span>: </span><span id="S2.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">An illustration of the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMoE</abbr> model for two image modalities. Each modality has one modality-specific encoder consisting of <math id="S2.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{MS}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>E</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{MS}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> Transformer-<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> layers and one modality-specific decoder consisting of <math id="S2.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{MS}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>D</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{MS}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> Transformer layers. Additionally, all modalities share a cross-sensor encoder consisting of <math id="S2.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{CS}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>E</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{CS}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> Transformer-<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> layers. Different colors indicate the processing pathways for each <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RS</abbr> image modality.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To simultaneously address the computational complexity limitations of masked autoencoder–based models while preserving cross-modal representation learning, we introduce a general adaptation for efficient multi-modal processing.
Although the proposed adaptation can be applied to any transformer-based <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FM</abbr>, in this paper we apply the adaptation to <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMAE</abbr>, resulting in the  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMoE</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMoE</abbr>) model, the first compute-efficient multi-modal <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FM</abbr> for <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RS</abbr>.
The proposed <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMoE</abbr> model extends <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMAE</abbr> by integrating Soft <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> mechanisms into the encoder components, enabling selective expert activation while maintaining both intra-modal and inter-modal characteristics.
To achieve this, we adapt <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMAE</abbr> by: i) integrating expert routing; ii) adapting the encoder architecture; and iii) including regularizing training objectives.
<a href="#S2.F1" title="In II-B The proposed CSMoE Model ‣ II Proposed Efficient Remote Sensing Foundation Model with Soft Mixture-of-Experts ‣ CSMoE: An Efficient Remote Sensing Foundation Model with Soft Mixture-of-Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows an illustration of the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMoE</abbr> model, while our adaptations are explained in detail in the following.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.14104v1/figures/images/SoftRSMoE.drawio_v07.90.jpg" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:467/318;" width="467" height="318" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Qualitative example of the dispatch process. Slots (<math id="S2.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1},\,...\,s_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1},\,...\,s_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>) are formed as a weighted combination of the input features (<math id="S2.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{1},\,\dots\,z_{49}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>49</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{1},\,\dots\,z_{49}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=49" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>49</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=49</annotation></semantics></math> patches) and assigned to experts (<math id="S2.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1},\,\dots\,E_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1},\,\dots\,E_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>). The dispatch weights <math id="S2.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{3,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{3,n}</annotation></semantics></math> for slot 3 are shown in red, features without annotation have a zero-weight. Note, that in the model this process happens in the embedding space but for visualization it is shown in the image space.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMoE</abbr> model leverages Soft <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> to reduce computational complexity while maintaining model capacity through selective expert activation.
Unlike sparse <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> that route individual tokens to top-<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> out of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> experts, Soft <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> employs a two-stage routing mechanism based on <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> intermediate representations called slots <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\boldsymbol{s}_{\vartheta}\}_{\vartheta=1}^{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>ϑ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\boldsymbol{s}_{\vartheta}\}_{\vartheta=1}^{S}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As shown in <a href="#S2.F2" title="In II-B The proposed CSMoE Model ‣ II Proposed Efficient Remote Sensing Foundation Model with Soft Mixture-of-Experts ‣ CSMoE: An Efficient Remote Sensing Foundation Model with Soft Mixture-of-Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, each slot summarizes a subset of input features through weighted aggregation, where slot–feature similarities are converted into dispatch weights <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\vartheta,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\vartheta,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and combine weights <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\alpha}_{\vartheta,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\alpha}_{\vartheta,n}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\vartheta,n}=\text{softmax}_{n}\left(\frac{\hat{s}_{\vartheta,n}}{\tau}\right),\quad\hat{\alpha}_{\vartheta,n}=\text{softmax}_{\vartheta}(\hat{s}_{\vartheta,n})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>softmax</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mi>τ</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>softmax</mtext><mi>ϑ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\vartheta,n}=\text{softmax}_{n}\left(\frac{\hat{s}_{\vartheta,n}}{\tau}\right),\quad\hat{\alpha}_{\vartheta,n}=\text{softmax}_{\vartheta}(\hat{s}_{\vartheta,n}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is a temperature parameter, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{softmax}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>softmax</mtext><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{softmax}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{softmax}_{\vartheta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>softmax</mtext><mi>ϑ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{softmax}_{\vartheta}</annotation></semantics></math> are softmax operations along the token and slot dimensions, respectively and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{s}_{\vartheta,n}\}_{n=1}^{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>P</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{s}_{\vartheta,n}\}_{n=1}^{P}</annotation></semantics></math> are slot embeddings (learned projections of the input features <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{z_{n}\}_{n=1}^{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>P</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{z_{n}\}_{n=1}^{P}</annotation></semantics></math>) for expert <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vartheta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϑ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vartheta</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each slot <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\vartheta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>ϑ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\vartheta}</annotation></semantics></math> is formed as a weighted average of the input features as:</p>
<table id="S2.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\vartheta}=\sum_{n=1}^{P}\alpha_{\vartheta,n}\cdot z_{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>ϑ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>P</mi></munderover><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\vartheta}=\sum_{n=1}^{P}\alpha_{\vartheta,n}\cdot z_{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">The slots are then processed by one assigned expert each and the final outputs are reconstructed through weighted aggregation.
This approach reduces expert calls from <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\cdot P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\cdot P</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, enabling efficient processing while preserving representational capacity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMoE</abbr> model incorporates Soft <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> into both encoder components of <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMAE</abbr>.
The multi-sensor encoder employs modality-specific <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> layers, where separate expert networks with different parameters are utilized for different image modalities, allowing accurate modeling of sensor-specific characteristics.
Additionally, the cross-sensor encoder employs shared <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> layers, where the same expert networks process features from all modalities to facilitate inter-modal pattern learning.
Based on this, we define the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMoE</abbr> model as follows:</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span>1 </span>Modality-Specific Encoding</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each image modality is processed through its dedicated encoder consisting of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{MS}}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mtext>MS</mtext><mi>E</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{MS}}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> Soft <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> transformer encoder layers.
For example, for modality <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, the unmasked tokens <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{U}}^{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{U}}^{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> are processed using it’s modality-specific encoder <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{MS}^{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{MS}^{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S2.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}_{\text{MS}}^{\mathcal{X}}=e_{MS}^{\mathcal{X}}(\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{U}}^{\mathcal{X}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mtext>MS</mtext><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}_{\text{MS}}^{\mathcal{X}}=e_{MS}^{\mathcal{X}}(\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{U}}^{\mathcal{X}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where each encoder layer applies self-attention followed by Soft <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> processing.
For modality <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, the processing is identical using its respective modality-specific encoder <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{MS}^{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{MS}^{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
These modality-specific encoders enable expert specialization for different semantic patterns within each image modality.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span>2 </span>Cross-Sensor Encoding</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The outputs from both modality-specific encoders are processed through a shared cross-sensor encoder <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{CS}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{CS}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{CS}}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mtext>CS</mtext><mi>E</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{CS}}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> Soft <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> transformer encoder layers:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{Z}_{\text{CS}}^{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mtext>CS</mtext><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{Z}_{\text{CS}}^{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=e_{CS}(\mathcal{Z}_{\text{MS}}^{\mathcal{X}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mtext>MS</mtext><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=e_{CS}(\mathcal{Z}_{\text{MS}}^{\mathcal{X}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{Z}_{\text{CS}}^{\mathcal{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mtext>CS</mtext><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{Z}_{\text{CS}}^{\mathcal{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=e_{CS}(\mathcal{Z}_{\text{MS}}^{\mathcal{Y}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mtext>MS</mtext><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=e_{CS}(\mathcal{Z}_{\text{MS}}^{\mathcal{Y}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The shared Soft <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> transformer encoder layers learn to route cross-modal patterns to appropriate experts, facilitating inter-modal relationship modeling while maintaining computational efficiency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span>3 </span>Modality-Specific Decoding</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For reconstruction, separate decoders <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{MS}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{MS}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{MS}}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mtext>MS</mtext><mi>D</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{MS}}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> standard transformer layers are employed.
The decoders process cross-sensor representations to reconstruct both unimodal and cross-modal targets:</p>
<table id="S2.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{P}}_{\mathcal{M}}^{j\leftarrow j^{\prime}}=\text{Linear}_{j\leftarrow j^{\prime}}(d_{MS}^{j}(\mathcal{Z}_{\text{CS}}^{j^{\prime}},\boldsymbol{z}_{m}^{j}))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>Linear</mtext><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mtext>CS</mtext><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{P}}_{\mathcal{M}}^{j\leftarrow j^{\prime}}=\text{Linear}_{j\leftarrow j^{\prime}}(d_{MS}^{j}(\mathcal{Z}_{\text{CS}}^{j^{\prime}},\boldsymbol{z}_{m}^{j})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j,j^{\prime}\in\{\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j,j^{\prime}\in\{\mathcal{X},\mathcal{Y}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\leftarrow j^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\leftarrow j^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the reconstruction of the image modality <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> using features of the image modality <math id="S2.SS2.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The decoders do not include <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MoE</abbr> components as they are removed after pretraining.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS4.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span>4 </span>Training Objective Extensions</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To regularize expert routing and ensure stable training, we extend the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMAE</abbr> training objectives with two additional loss functions.
The slot repulsion loss <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{REP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>REP</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{REP}}</annotation></semantics></math> encourages reduced correlation between slot embeddings:</p>
<table id="S2.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{REP}}=-\frac{1}{S^{2}}\sum_{\vartheta=1}^{S}\sum_{\vartheta^{\prime}=1}^{S}\left(\langle\tilde{s}_{\vartheta},\tilde{s}_{\vartheta^{\prime}}\rangle\right)^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>REP</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></munderover><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>ϑ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{REP}}=-\frac{1}{S^{2}}\sum_{\vartheta=1}^{S}\sum_{\vartheta^{\prime}=1}^{S}\left(\langle\tilde{s}_{\vartheta},\tilde{s}_{\vartheta^{\prime}}\rangle\right)^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">while the entropy loss <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{ENT}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>ENT</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{ENT}}</annotation></semantics></math> promotes balanced expert usage:</p>
<table id="S2.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{ENT}}=-\frac{1}{SP}\sum_{\vartheta=1}^{S}\sum_{n=1}^{P}\alpha_{\vartheta,n}\log\left(\alpha_{\vartheta,n}+\varepsilon\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>ENT</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></munderover><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>P</mi></munderover><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{ENT}}=-\frac{1}{SP}\sum_{\vartheta=1}^{S}\sum_{n=1}^{P}\alpha_{\vartheta,n}\log\left(\alpha_{\vartheta,n}+\varepsilon\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is a small term for numerical stability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The overall training objective combines the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CSMAE</abbr> losses with regularization terms:</p>
<table id="S2.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{UMR}}+\mathcal{L}_{\text{CMR}}+\mathcal{L}_{\text{MI}}+\lambda\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\text{REP}}+\gamma\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\text{ENT}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>UMR</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>CMR</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MI</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>REP</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>ENT</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{UMR}}+\mathcal{L}_{\text{CMR}}+\mathcal{L}_{\text{MI}}+\lambda\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\text{REP}}+\gamma\cdot\mathcal{L}_{\text{ENT}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> are scaling parameters controlling the regularization influence.
In this way, the characterization of cross-modal <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RS</abbr> image representations is achieved by learning to reconstruct both intra-modal and inter-modal image content while maintaining computational efficiency through selective expert activation and promoting stable expert usage through regularized routing.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">II-C</span> </span><span id="S2.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A Thematic-Climatic Descriptor-Driven Sampling Strategy</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The efficiency of <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FM</abbr>-training in <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RS</abbr> depends not only on model design choices, but also on training data selection.
The use of global datasets often introduces substantial redundancy, where vast amounts of data contribute little additional information.
For instance, when training on global coverage, more than 70% of the images may depict oceans, which are irrelevant for most land-focused applications.
Even within land areas, extended homogeneous regions, such as the Amazon rainforest, the Siberian taiga, or the Sahara desert, dominate the dataset but provide limited additional information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To address this issue, we propose a thematic-climatic descriptor-driven sampling strategy that samples a reduced but representative training set from a large-scale <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RS</abbr> data archive.
This strategy efficiently samples a training set with a wide range of geographic and thematic-climatic variability.
The strategy consists of two stages:
i) auxiliary descriptor generation, where each image is associated with climatic and thematic descriptors derived from global climate zone and thematic product datasets; and
ii) entropy-maximizing stratified sampling, where a genetic algorithm selects spatially dispersed and thematically-climatically balanced samples.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-C</span>1 </span>Auxiliary Descriptor Generation</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{\varphi_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{\varphi_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of bounding boxes (the set of coordinates that define the spatial extend) of the images within a large-scale <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RS</abbr> data archive <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> that consists of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> images or co-registered image tuples, where <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the longitude and latitude coordinates of the bounding box of the <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th image of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We assume that the images are unlabeled, i.e., no thematic (e.g., <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">LULC</abbr>) or climatic information is directly available.
In the first stage, each image is associated with a thematic and a climatic descriptor derived from global raster layers.
Specifically, each bounding box <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is linked to:
i) a climate zone raster <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{climate}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>climate</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{climate}}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., Köppen–Geiger <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>), which provides coarse climatic information; and
ii) a thematic land-cover product <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{TP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>TP</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{TP}}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., ESA WorldCover <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>), which captures land-use properties at finer scale.
This combination allows us to incorporate both climate and local land-cover information, two complementary dimensions of variability, into the sampling process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In detail, each bounding box <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is associated with a climate stratum <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and a thematic stratum <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, depending on the overlay with the reference rasters.
The resulting set of tuples is therefore represented as <math id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D^{\prime}}=\{(\varphi_{i},u_{i},v_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N^{\prime}}\;|\;N^{\prime}\leq N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D^{\prime}}=\{(\varphi_{i},u_{i},v_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N^{\prime}}\;|\;N^{\prime}\leq N</annotation></semantics></math>, where only images covered by both rasters are retained.
We refer to these thematic-climatic strata as descriptors since they are not manually labeled but derived from external data sources.
These descriptors are used for the subsequent stratified sampling stage by embedding thematic and climatic variability directly into the sampling process.
The algorithm of the descriptor generation stage is given in <a href="#alg1" title="In II-C1 Auxiliary Descriptor Generation ‣ II-C A Thematic-Climatic Descriptor-Driven Sampling Strategy ‣ II Proposed Efficient Remote Sensing Foundation Model with Soft Mixture-of-Experts ‣ CSMoE: An Efficient Remote Sensing Foundation Model with Soft Mixture-of-Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Assign Thematic and Climatic Descriptors to Coordinates</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
                
                
              Set of coordinates <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{\varphi_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{\varphi_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
                
                
              Climate map <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{climate}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>climate</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{climate}}</annotation></semantics></math> with bounds <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{climate}}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mtext>climate</mtext><mi>B</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{climate}}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
                
                
              Thematic product map <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{TP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>TP</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{TP}}</annotation></semantics></math> with bounds <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{TP}}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mtext>TP</mtext><mi>B</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{TP}}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
                
                
              Descriptors for each coordinate: climate stratum <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and thematic product stratum <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
                
                
              Initialize empty annotated coordinates <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D^{\prime}}=\{\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D^{\prime}}=\{\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each coordinate <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}\in\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}\in\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
                
                
                <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}\not\subset M_{\text{climate}}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊄</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mtext>climate</mtext><mi>B</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}\not\subset M_{\text{climate}}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{i}\not\subset M_{\text{TP}}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊄</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mtext>TP</mtext><mi>B</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{i}\not\subset M_{\text{TP}}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
                
                
                 Continue to next coordinate

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
                
                
                <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}\leftarrow M_{\text{climate}}[\varphi_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>climate</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}\leftarrow M_{\text{climate}}[\varphi_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}\leftarrow M_{\text{TP}}[\varphi_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>TP</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}\leftarrow M_{\text{TP}}[\varphi_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
                
                
                Add <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varphi_{i},u_{i},v_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varphi_{i},u_{i},v_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> Set of pseudo-annotated tuples <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS3.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-C</span>2 </span>Entropy-Maximizing Stratified Sampling</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the set of tuples <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> containing locations and descriptors, the entropy-maximizing stratified sampling aims to construct a reduced training set <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> that preserves the spatial and thematic-climatic variability of <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> while minimizing redundancies among the selected samples.
To this end, we stratify <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> by joined thematic and climatic descriptors and then sample within each joined stratum using an entropy-driven sampling process.
Specifically, for each pair <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math> of climatic and thematic strata, we define:</p>
<table id="S2.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E21.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{u,v}=\left\{(\varphi_{i},u_{i},v_{i})\in\mathcal{D^{\prime}}\;\middle|\;u_{i}=u,\;v_{i}=v\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{u,v}=\left\{(\varphi_{i},u_{i},v_{i})\in\mathcal{D^{\prime}}\;\middle|\;u_{i}=u,\;v_{i}=v\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">While some joined strata are small and can be fully retained (i.e., the size of the joined stratum is smaller than a user-defined target sample count per stratum <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>), others are extensive and can be sampled from to reduce redundancy.
In contrast to random sampling, we use the spatial dispersion (distance) of selected samples within each joined stratum as a criterion for sampling.
To this end, we employ a genetic algorithm that iteratively optimizes a subset of samples within <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{u,v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{u,v}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each candidate solution <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{S}_{u,v}\mid\boldsymbol{b}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒃</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{S}_{u,v}\mid\boldsymbol{b}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of samples, where <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}</annotation></semantics></math> is a binary mask indicating which samples of the joined stratum are selected for the candidate solution.
The fitness of the candidate solution is measured by an entropy-based score that uses the pairwise Haversine distances.
This encourages the selection of samples that are representative and spatially diverse.
Standard evolutionary operators are used to optimize candidate solutions.
Additionally, to maintain the target sample counts per joined stratum <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use an adaptive constraint mechanism: if a candidate solution exceeds <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="110\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>110</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">110\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the target sample count, it is randomly pruned; if it falls below <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>90</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90\%</annotation></semantics></math> it is randomly augmented.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">After convergence, or if a given compute budget is reached, the candidate solution with the highest fitness is selected.
The process is repeated independently across all joined strata, and the union of candidate solutions with the highest respective fitness defines the training set <math id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This strategy ensures that the resulting training set is spatially well-dispersed, thematically and climatically balanced, and substantially less redundant than the original archive.
The algorithm of the optimization stage is given in <a href="#alg2" title="In II-C2 Entropy-Maximizing Stratified Sampling ‣ II-C A Thematic-Climatic Descriptor-Driven Sampling Strategy ‣ II Proposed Efficient Remote Sensing Foundation Model with Soft Mixture-of-Experts ‣ CSMoE: An Efficient Remote Sensing Foundation Model with Soft Mixture-of-Experts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Entropy-Based Subsampling via Genetic Optimization</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
                
                
              Set of location-descriptor tuples <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
                
                
              Number of samples per joined stratum <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, number of iterations <math id="alg2.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, population size <math id="alg2.l2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, climate zones <math id="alg2.l2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, thematic product classes <math id="alg2.l2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
                
                
              Mutation rate <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, crossover rate <math id="alg2.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
                
                
              Optimized stratified sample subset <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> with high spatial entropy

</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
                
                
              Initialize empty sample subset <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\star}=\{\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\star}=\{\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg2.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for all</span> climatic strata <math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in C_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in C_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
                
                
                <span id="alg2.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for all</span> thematic strata <math id="alg2.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in C_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in C_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
                
                
                 Extract subset <math id="alg2.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{u,v}\subseteq\mathcal{D^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{u,v}\subseteq\mathcal{D^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="alg2.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}=u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}=u</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg2.l8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}=v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}=v</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
                
                
                 <math id="alg2.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{g}\leftarrow|\mathcal{S}_{u,v}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{g}\leftarrow|\mathcal{S}_{u,v}|</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
                
                
                 <span id="alg2.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg2.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{g}\leq N_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{g}\leq N_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
                
                
                   Add all elements of <math id="alg2.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{u,v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{u,v}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg2.l11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
                
                
                 <span id="alg2.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
                
                
                   Define objective function <math id="alg2.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{g}(\boldsymbol{b})\leftarrow\text{Entropy}(\{\mathcal{S}_{u,v}\mid\boldsymbol{b}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒃</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>Entropy</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝒃</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{g}(\boldsymbol{b})\leftarrow\text{Entropy}(\{\mathcal{S}_{u,v}\mid\boldsymbol{b}\})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
                
                
                   Run genetic algorithm <math id="alg2.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{b}^{\star}\leftarrow\text{GA}(f_{g},n_{g},T,N_{p},N_{s},r_{m},r_{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext>GA</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{b}^{\star}\leftarrow\text{GA}(f_{g},n_{g},T,N_{p},N_{s},r_{m},r_{c})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
                
                
